WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.459 --> 00:00:08.129
One of the most evident remnants
of colonialism on the African continent

00:00:08.171 --> 00:00:11.716
are laws that are used to persecute people
for the sexual orientation,

00:00:11.758 --> 00:00:14.261
gender identity and gender expression.

00:00:14.302 --> 00:00:16.930
I want to tell you about some
sections of the penal code

00:00:16.972 --> 00:00:18.598
from my home country, Kenya.

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:22.143
Section 162 (a)
and (c) of the penal code

00:00:22.185 --> 00:00:25.772
say that it is a felony for any person
to have carnal knowledge

00:00:25.814 --> 00:00:28.400
of any other person
against the order of nature,

00:00:28.441 --> 00:00:32.112
or to permit a male person
to have carnal knowledge of him or her

00:00:32.112 --> 00:00:33.488
against the order of nature.

00:00:33.530 --> 00:00:36.366
And this is punishable
by 14 years imprisonment.

00:00:36.408 --> 00:00:38.535
Section 165 of the Penal Code

00:00:38.577 --> 00:00:40.537
sees that it's a felony for a male person

00:00:40.579 --> 00:00:45.125
to commit any act of gross indecency
with another male person,

00:00:45.166 --> 00:00:46.835
whether in public or in private.

00:00:46.835 --> 00:00:50.046
And this is punishable
by five years imprisonment.

00:00:50.088 --> 00:00:55.135
Right now, similar laws also exist
in 35 of 56 Commonwealth countries

00:00:55.176 --> 00:00:57.762
and more than 60 countries
across the world.

00:00:57.804 --> 00:00:59.139
These laws inform attacks,

00:00:59.139 --> 00:01:02.601
arbitrary arrests and degrade
queer people's right to privacy.

00:01:02.892 --> 00:01:04.436
But they've done more than that.

00:01:04.477 --> 00:01:07.814
They've created a culture of reporting
on your friends, your neighbors,

00:01:07.856 --> 00:01:08.982
even your own family.

00:01:09.024 --> 00:01:10.734
And just a suspicion of being queer

00:01:10.734 --> 00:01:13.069
can result in these
life-threatening consequences.

00:01:13.111 --> 00:01:15.363
So as you might imagine,
under these conditions,

00:01:15.363 --> 00:01:18.700
many queer people are constantly
operating from a place of concern

00:01:18.742 --> 00:01:20.535
and fear about being outed.

00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:22.329
And if we're all in hiding,

00:01:22.329 --> 00:01:25.665
then how can we mobilize around issues
that impede our everyday lives

00:01:25.707 --> 00:01:27.584
or share resources in times of need?

00:01:27.626 --> 00:01:30.837
How can we create a space
where we can thrive?

00:01:31.588 --> 00:01:35.300
We hear a lot in the news lately
about social media and online platforms

00:01:35.342 --> 00:01:37.927
as spaces where serious harm can occur,

00:01:37.969 --> 00:01:39.888
but what I want to share with you today

00:01:39.888 --> 00:01:42.349
is how social networks
can be designed intentionally

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:44.976
to build community
and trust from the ground up.

00:01:45.018 --> 00:01:46.895
And if they're built correctly,

00:01:46.936 --> 00:01:48.772
then they can help counteract the fear

00:01:48.813 --> 00:01:51.358
that's been entrenched
by colonial systems and culture

00:01:51.358 --> 00:01:52.776
in so many African countries.

00:01:52.776 --> 00:01:54.402
I want to share with you the ways

00:01:54.402 --> 00:01:56.529
that queer communities
are using social media

00:01:56.571 --> 00:02:00.367
to protect one another
and be themselves, queer and visible.

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:03.036
When I created my first
social media account

00:02:03.078 --> 00:02:05.497
and joined a queer online community,

00:02:05.538 --> 00:02:07.999
I met people who created
spaces that embraced

00:02:07.999 --> 00:02:11.002
and protected everyone's ability
to be their authentic self.

00:02:11.044 --> 00:02:14.464
And this was the first time
that I genuinely felt safe.

00:02:14.506 --> 00:02:15.840
Just one example.

00:02:15.882 --> 00:02:19.010
Blackmail is a huge problem
for the queer community in Kenya.

00:02:19.052 --> 00:02:21.262
Blackmailers can make tons of money

00:02:21.262 --> 00:02:24.140
by threatening to out queer
people to authorities.

00:02:24.182 --> 00:02:27.185
So one queer online community
that I joined when I was younger

00:02:27.227 --> 00:02:30.230
was trying to prevent
and expose known blackmailers.

00:02:30.271 --> 00:02:33.525
I realized that one of the people
listed as a blackmailer

00:02:33.525 --> 00:02:35.777
was an individual I had been talking to.

00:02:35.819 --> 00:02:36.986
And this group saved me

00:02:36.986 --> 00:02:39.155
from a potentially
life-threatening situation.

00:02:39.155 --> 00:02:41.199
But for a lot of other people in the group

00:02:41.241 --> 00:02:42.450
it was already too late.

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:46.246
They'd already been scammed and harmed
by someone who targeted them

00:02:46.287 --> 00:02:48.081
because they were queer.

00:02:48.707 --> 00:02:50.625
Later on in life, I met Nerima,

00:02:50.625 --> 00:02:54.379
a nonbinary lesbian who, similar to me,
found that in internet spaces

00:02:54.379 --> 00:02:56.881
they could be their authentic
selves safely.

00:02:56.923 --> 00:02:59.509
In their hometown, men would grab, spit,

00:02:59.509 --> 00:03:01.428
take unsolicited videos of them

00:03:01.469 --> 00:03:04.013
because of how they express
their gender in public.

00:03:04.055 --> 00:03:07.809
But online Nerima could develop
many versions of themselves.

00:03:07.809 --> 00:03:09.018
For me and Nerima,

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:11.771
the internet was the only place
where we could talk openly

00:03:11.813 --> 00:03:12.981
and support each other

00:03:13.022 --> 00:03:15.650
through some of the most difficult
moments in our lives.

00:03:15.650 --> 00:03:18.820
We helped each other and people
in our community find safe housing,

00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:20.405
navigate loss after being outed.

00:03:20.405 --> 00:03:21.573
We've hosted memorials

00:03:21.614 --> 00:03:24.868
and helped each other find
queer family experts when we needed them.

00:03:24.868 --> 00:03:28.163
These virtual networks
saved and sustained us,

00:03:28.204 --> 00:03:30.498
and these experiences led
Nerima and me to create

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:33.126
The Queer Africa Network, QAN,

00:03:33.168 --> 00:03:37.589
an online platform where we hope
to ensure that every queer African,

00:03:37.630 --> 00:03:40.216
both on the African continent
and in the diaspora,

00:03:40.258 --> 00:03:42.594
has access to the resources they need,

00:03:42.594 --> 00:03:45.388
like verified opportunities, mentorship,

00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:47.348
fundraising and safety.

00:03:47.724 --> 00:03:48.892
Over the years,

00:03:48.933 --> 00:03:52.395
we've learned a lot about how to build
a safe online community.

00:03:52.395 --> 00:03:54.981
And I want to share
those learnings with you today.

00:03:55.356 --> 00:03:57.692
The first key is community vetting.

00:03:58.193 --> 00:04:02.197
Any safe online space has to find
a way to keep out the bots,

00:04:02.238 --> 00:04:03.990
the trolls and the blackmailers.

00:04:03.990 --> 00:04:05.992
And we found that the best way to do this

00:04:05.992 --> 00:04:08.912
is to implement a three-week
probation period for new members.

00:04:08.953 --> 00:04:12.040
Anyone joining the community
can engage with limited features

00:04:12.081 --> 00:04:15.627
as we assess their activity and ensure
that accounts haven't been reported

00:04:15.668 --> 00:04:18.087
for violating community guidelines.

00:04:18.129 --> 00:04:19.506
After the probation period,

00:04:19.547 --> 00:04:23.134
a member can submit a request
to have a verified mark on their profile.

00:04:23.176 --> 00:04:24.844
This indicates to other members

00:04:24.886 --> 00:04:28.598
that this profile is safe to admit
to their private groups and events.

00:04:28.598 --> 00:04:31.351
Safety is essential above all else.

00:04:31.726 --> 00:04:33.520
Once safety can be established,

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:35.563
support is the next essential feature.

00:04:35.605 --> 00:04:37.857
We think about support very intentionally.

00:04:37.899 --> 00:04:39.067
For queer Africans,

00:04:39.067 --> 00:04:42.987
financial stability plays a huge role
in determining how open we can be.

00:04:43.029 --> 00:04:45.615
And over the years, I've met
queer Africans who believe

00:04:45.657 --> 00:04:47.742
that the only way
they can get ahead at work

00:04:47.742 --> 00:04:49.410
is by abandoning their identities

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:51.287
and anyone who shares those identities.

00:04:51.329 --> 00:04:52.789
That's a terrible feeling.

00:04:52.789 --> 00:04:55.583
And of course, anyone
suspected of being queer

00:04:55.625 --> 00:04:57.544
might not be able to work at all.

00:04:57.585 --> 00:04:59.504
So in order to counteract this,

00:04:59.546 --> 00:05:03.341
it's important to design a space
with an active opportunities hub

00:05:03.383 --> 00:05:04.634
and marketplace,

00:05:04.676 --> 00:05:07.637
one that helps people
find community, connections

00:05:07.679 --> 00:05:10.265
and mentorship in their various
fields and industries.

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:12.809
This moves them closer
to financial stability,

00:05:12.809 --> 00:05:15.395
and they can trade their arts
and products in new ways,

00:05:15.436 --> 00:05:17.856
not just for money,
but through barter as well.

00:05:17.897 --> 00:05:20.108
We're challenging
the core tenets of capitalism

00:05:20.108 --> 00:05:21.734
that are rooted in individualism,

00:05:21.776 --> 00:05:23.987
capital accumulation and competition

00:05:23.987 --> 00:05:25.738
by creating a behavior change

00:05:25.738 --> 00:05:27.407
and system level change

00:05:27.448 --> 00:05:29.909
that prioritizes collaboration,

00:05:29.951 --> 00:05:32.203
resource sharing and community.

00:05:33.371 --> 00:05:35.623
One of the key consequences of colonialism

00:05:35.623 --> 00:05:38.877
was erasing and replacing
precolonial African history.

00:05:38.918 --> 00:05:42.171
And this made us lose stories of love,
alternative social structures,

00:05:42.171 --> 00:05:45.842
and a myriad of language
to describe our identities.

00:05:45.842 --> 00:05:47.176
More than erasing the past,

00:05:47.218 --> 00:05:49.596
it's hindered the possibility
of new futures.

00:05:49.971 --> 00:05:51.139
Because of this,

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:53.016
one of the core necessary features

00:05:53.057 --> 00:05:57.228
that we found in designing a positive
and thriving online social community

00:05:57.228 --> 00:05:59.731
is the ability for members
to create a global archive

00:05:59.731 --> 00:06:01.816
of their diverse lived experiences.

00:06:01.816 --> 00:06:04.736
To document the stories
of those who never got to tell theirs,

00:06:04.777 --> 00:06:07.113
and to collect vital
statistical information

00:06:07.113 --> 00:06:10.783
from the community on where resources
and support are needed.

00:06:10.825 --> 00:06:14.162
Features such as blogs,
archives and surveys

00:06:14.162 --> 00:06:19.542
give us the opportunity to document
our past and articulate our future

00:06:19.542 --> 00:06:22.378
and to share where direct
resources are needed.

00:06:23.212 --> 00:06:28.343
There's an immense gap on the research
on the experiences of queer Africans,

00:06:28.384 --> 00:06:32.013
which is often weaponized against us
to suggest that we don't exist.

00:06:32.347 --> 00:06:35.308
If governments aren't collecting
information on suicide rates

00:06:35.350 --> 00:06:36.559
and homelessness,

00:06:36.559 --> 00:06:38.645
then they can argue
that if there's no data,

00:06:38.686 --> 00:06:39.854
there's no issue.

00:06:39.896 --> 00:06:42.231
Regardless of how often
these laws are applied,

00:06:42.231 --> 00:06:44.817
they have contributed
to the lack of information

00:06:44.817 --> 00:06:48.905
on how many queer people are barred
from accessing lifesaving services,

00:06:48.947 --> 00:06:53.493
spanning education, health,
legal assistance programs and more.

00:06:53.910 --> 00:06:56.162
But imagine for a moment
if you could do this

00:06:56.204 --> 00:06:59.332
in a way that allows us
to own our own data

00:06:59.374 --> 00:07:02.085
in a secure, encrypted
and consensual manner

00:07:02.126 --> 00:07:03.753
that is not extractive.

00:07:03.753 --> 00:07:06.631
We'd be able to know how many of us
are forced out of schools

00:07:06.673 --> 00:07:08.174
by homophobia and transphobia.

00:07:08.216 --> 00:07:10.385
How many of us
are grappling with blackmail,

00:07:10.426 --> 00:07:12.679
how many of us feel forced
to end our lives

00:07:12.679 --> 00:07:14.514
or how many of us seek asylum

00:07:14.555 --> 00:07:17.725
because we can't imagine ourselves
living a happy, fulfilling life

00:07:17.725 --> 00:07:19.268
in our country of birth?

00:07:19.644 --> 00:07:20.812
Through our platform,

00:07:20.853 --> 00:07:24.440
members can report and record
these experiences accurately.

00:07:24.482 --> 00:07:27.402
We can co-create the infrastructure
to address these issues

00:07:27.443 --> 00:07:28.820
and protect each other.

00:07:29.320 --> 00:07:31.990
Now, QAN is just one
of many online platforms

00:07:32.031 --> 00:07:36.202
trying to rebuild and design
this new kind of queer-owned online space.

00:07:36.202 --> 00:07:38.079
And as we continue creating this space,

00:07:38.121 --> 00:07:41.290
we're standing on the shoulders
of all those who've come before us.

00:07:41.332 --> 00:07:43.334
And while I don't think safe online spaces

00:07:43.334 --> 00:07:45.920
can solve all the problems
that queer communities face,

00:07:45.962 --> 00:07:48.673
I do think that being able
to gather safely in community,

00:07:48.673 --> 00:07:50.675
collect stories and share resources

00:07:50.675 --> 00:07:52.844
is leading to noteworthy changes.

00:07:52.885 --> 00:07:54.012
For example,

00:07:54.053 --> 00:07:57.390
most of the queer organizations
operating across the African continent,

00:07:57.432 --> 00:08:00.601
especially in contexts
where it’s illegal to be queer,

00:08:00.643 --> 00:08:03.479
use social media
to identify people in need,

00:08:03.521 --> 00:08:07.525
organize and survey their constituents
in order to know what is going on.

00:08:07.567 --> 00:08:08.735
And over the past years,

00:08:08.735 --> 00:08:11.863
we’re seeing waves of decriminalization
of same-sex intimacy laws,

00:08:11.863 --> 00:08:13.281
and we're seeing new momentum

00:08:13.322 --> 00:08:15.283
and unprecedented levels of acceptance.

00:08:15.283 --> 00:08:17.535
Though there's still a long way to go.

00:08:17.535 --> 00:08:20.538
In the last decade, we've seen
these changes across Africa,

00:08:20.538 --> 00:08:24.250
Angola, Botswana, Seychelles,
Mozambique and Lesotho.

00:08:24.250 --> 00:08:27.045
We've also seen decriminalization
outside of the continent,

00:08:27.045 --> 00:08:28.588
such as in Antigua and Barbuda,

00:08:28.588 --> 00:08:31.758
which decriminalized
homosexuality in July of 2022.

00:08:32.175 --> 00:08:34.052
More than keeping queer people safe,

00:08:34.052 --> 00:08:36.763
these approaches show us
what our future could look like.

00:08:36.804 --> 00:08:39.974
A world where we're safe online
and thriving offline.

00:08:39.974 --> 00:08:41.184
Thank you.

